description:
I am looking into changing `stats::delete.response` so that under R >= 4.7 it deletes `dataClasses[response]`.  Checking all 708 CRAN/BioC packages that access `dataClasses` or call `delete.response` (according to `grep`) suggests that only a few packages would need revision, tentatively just **mgcv** and **hardhat**.

It would help if **hardhat** passed its check whether `stats::delete.response` deletes `dataClasses[response]` or not.  I advised the maintainer of **mgcv** to use a `.onLoad` like:
```
.onLoad <- function(libname, pkgname) {
    if (length(attr(delete.response(terms(model.frame(y ~ 1, data.frame(y = 0)))), "dataClasses"))) {
        ## stats::delete.response does not delete dataClasses[response]
        ## in this version of R.  Once R version x.y is patched, the test
        ## can be changed to 'if (getRversion() < "x.y")', for speed.
        assign("delete.response",
               function (termobj) {
                   ans <- stats::delete.response(termobj)
                   if (r <- attr(termobj, "response"))
                       attr(ans, "dataClasses") <- attr(termobj, "dataClasses")[-r]
                   ans
               },
               parent.env(environment()))
    }
}
```
**hardhat** could do similarly then define
```
delete_response <-
function (terms) {
    check_terms(terms)
    delete.response(terms)
}
```
Of course you are free to take a different approach.  Anyway, this issue is just a notice.

When we get to passing calls through the functions. I would appriciate if the following functions gains a `call` argument.

Used in {recipes}

- `validate_column_names()`
- `hardhat::recompose()`

Used in {yardstick}

- `weighted_table()`

Currently can provide an easy implementation that can be replaced with #84 

```
validate_outcomes_is_factor <- function(outcomes) {
  is_factor <- vapply(outcomes, is.factor, FUN.VALUE = logical(1))
  if (!all(is_factor)) {
    rlang::abort(paste0("The outcome must be a factor."))
  }
}
```

They aren't supported directly by `mold()`, but would be up to the modeler to use correctly. Maybe we can provide a validation function to ensure they are valid looking case weights (integer-ish, same length as `x`, etc)

```
linear_regression <- function(x, y, case_weights) {
  x <- mold(x, y)
  xx <- linear_regression_impl(x = x$predictors, y = x$outcomes, case_weights)
  linear_reg_obj(xx, pre = x$preprocessor)
}

predict.line_reg_obj <- function(object, new_data) {
  new_data <- forge(object$pre, new_data)
  ...
  spruce_()
}
```

It would be good to have an option to skip the novel levels check. For `applicable`, we want to test to see if there are new points that are different than the others and this check coerces to NA in those cases (and we lose all subsequent calculations to propagated NA values). 

Maybe only for the recipe case (since we have `step_novel()`)? 

@marlycormar
